 **Position of this paper.** (i) The gold is the experimental factor for object-level scores of free-form captions: each caption is matched once and scored against nested reference golds and instance or verified labels (Section 3.1). Earlier gold-quality studies change classification, relevance or probing labels; in concurrent work, Lu et al. (2026) hold predictions fixed and exchange officially released gold versions in a registered study of structured prediction, and report open-ended generation as outside their tested scope. (ii) The count control matches the number of mentioned objects on every image of released captions, where LeHaCE fits length curves and CCEval balances means. (iii) Every prediction is registered or labelled exploratory or post hoc, failures included (Table 1). Prior work knows that incomplete gold inflates hallucination scores (Rohrbach et al., 2018), more so for detailed captions (Feng et al., 2024), and that writers name objects selectively (Misra et al., 2016); this paper measures gold completeness as a controlled factor that can set the sign of F1's verbosity slope, and applies a size-matched random-deletion null, in the manner of tests of the selected-completely-at-random assumption (Molotkov and Artomov, 2023; Teisseyre et al., 2024), to quantify that reporting bias on PASCAL-50S and COCO val2017 (Sections 6.4 and 6.6). These statements are relative to the works in Table 2.1 and the others cited here. Table 2.1 rests on the main text of each work, except Rashidi et al. (2024), of which only the abstract was read, and S. Liu et al. (2025), read in part. The searches behind it were partial.<!-- pass 3 (2026-10-28): positioning paragraph (scorecard: novelty legibility, venue fit); every comparison restates a claim already made and cited earlier in this section; no new citation, no new number --><!-- rev2 (CC-181 W8): condensed; original paragraph moved verbatim to Supplementary S10 -->
